lthrough target.\n4. `switch` default cases and out-of-range values.\n5. Order of evaluation / order of guard checks where the result differs.\n6. Any C code that MUTATES caller-visible memory. Note especially `validate_sequence`'s `bool *bools = (bool*)sequence;` buffer aliasing. Does the Rust write the identical bytes to the identical offsets?\n7. The `#[no_mangle]` wrapper in lib.rs: does its `access_len` computation expose exactly the bytes the C reads for each `operation`? Is the NULL check ordered/behaved identically? Is the signature ABI-identical to `int process_decisions(char*, size_t, int, int)`?\n8. main.rs: does `c_fgets` match C `fgets(buf, 1024, stdin)` exactly (max bytes read, newline retention, NULL-on-EOF)? Does `c_atoi` match glibc `atoi` (= `(int)strtol(s, NULL, 10)`) exactly, including leading whitespace, signs, no-digits, and overflow saturation to LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN then truncation to int? Does the strlen/newline-strip logic match? Do stderr messages, stdout formatting and exit codes match?\n\nYou may compile and run experiments to confirm any suspicion. Useful:\n- A prebuilt C shared library exporting `process_decisions` is at /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-xsEvjl/translated_rust/c_build/libcdecisions.so\n- The C executable is at /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-xsEvjl/translated_rust/c_src/build/driver\n- The Rust executable is at /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-xsEvjl/translated_rust/target/debug/driver\n- You may write scratch C/shell files ONLY under $TMPDIR. DO NOT modify anything under c_src/, src/, or tests/. DO NOT run cargo build/test (another process owns the build lock) — just read, reason, and optionally compile your own scratch C programs with gcc and run the two prebuilt executables.\n\nOutput format: a numbered list of CONFIRMED divergences, each with (a) the C line numbers, (b) the Rust line numbers, (c) an exact concrete input that distinguishes them, (d) the C result and the Rust